---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import { site } from "../data/site";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Cookies and local storage"
  description="Which cookies this site sets, why there are no advertising trackers, what the host may set for security, and how to control cookies in your browser."
>
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: "Cookies" }]} />
  <section class="tight">
    <div class="wrap narrow prose">
      <h1>Cookies and local storage</h1>
      <p class="lead" style="font-size:1.05rem;color:var(--ink-2)">
        This site is a set of static pages. It has no account system, no cart and no personalisation, so it has
        very little reason to store anything in your browser — and it does not.
      </p>

      <h2>What is set today</h2>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Advertising cookies</th><td>None. There is no ad network, no retargeting pixel and no cross-site identifier on this site.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Analytics cookies</th><td>None. If aggregate analytics is added, it will be a cookieless service and this page will say so before it goes live.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Functional cookies</th><td>None. There is no login, no saved quote and no language preference to remember.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Security cookies</th><td>Possible. Our host (Cloudflare) may set a short-lived cookie to distinguish browsers from bots. It carries no marketing purpose.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>What "cookie" and "local storage" mean</h2>
      <p>
        A cookie is a small text file a site asks your browser to keep and to send back on later requests. Local
        storage is a similar store that stays on your device and is not transmitted automatically. Both are used by
        most commercial sites to remember sessions, carts and preferences — none of which exist here.
      </p>

      <h2>Third parties</h2>
      <p>
        Two kinds of third party can see a request to this site: the hosting provider that serves the pages, and the
        email or messaging provider you contact us through. Clicking an email link or a WhatsApp link takes you to a
        service governed by its own privacy policy, not this one.
      </p>
      <p>
        We do not embed social widgets, video players or comment systems, because each of those would introduce a
        tracker that we would then have to disclose.
      </p>

      <h2>If this changes</h2>
      <p>
        If analytics, embedded media or an advertising tag is ever added, this page will list the tool, its purpose
        and its retention period before the tag goes live. Where consent is legally required, it will be requested
        before the tag loads rather than after.
      </p>

      <h2>Controlling cookies yourself</h2>
      <p>
        Every current browser lets you block or delete cookies, and to block them per site. Because this site sets no
        functional cookies, blocking them will not break anything here. Instructions are in your browser's privacy or
        security settings, under "cookies and site data".
      </p>

      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <p>
        Email <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a> if you want to know what a request to this site
        actually stores. The answer is in our <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> as well, which covers the data
        we hold when you send an enquiry.
      </p>

      <p class="small">Last reviewed: November 2026.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
